        "question": {
          ":question/id": "37584b8e-1e53-57ac-9945-9716a93525e2",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3 y + 3$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-2)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2 x \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=4 y + 3 \\\\ \\nabla f(1,-2)&=\\left(2\\right)\\,i+\\left(-5\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(1,-2)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2+\\left(-5\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(1,-2)}{\\|\\nabla f(1,-2)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(2\\right)\\,i+\\left(-5\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{29}}{29}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.",
