          ":question/id": "9a9643af-a434-53c5-9217-451eee5f487b",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- e^{- x + y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + 2$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(- \\frac{\\pi}{3},0)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(- \\frac{\\pi}{3},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(- \\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(- \\frac{\\pi}{3},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(- \\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.",
